!script {{
  --/| ==========================================================================
  --/| Tutorial 09: Writing D&D 2024 Beacon Typed Collection Values
  --/|
  --/| PURPOSE
  --/| The Typed Collections tutorial showed how to find one Beacon record and
  --/| read a field from it. This tutorial takes the next step and changes one
  --/| existing field on one typed record.
  --/|
  --/| The first example uses the character's Walk Speed record because it gives us
  --/| a simple numeric value that is easy to change and restore. The second uses
  --/| the built-in Poisoned Condition to demonstrate a boolean typed write.
  --/|
  --/| HOW TO USE IT
  --/| 1. Select a D&D 2024 Beacon character that has a Walk speed.
  --/| 2. Run this ScriptCard.
  --/| 3. The tutorial temporarily changes Walk speed by 1.
  --/| 4. It reads the value back and then restores the exact starting value.
  --/|
  --/| WHAT THIS TEACHES
  --/| A typed collection path follows this pattern:
  --/|
  --/| collection -> selector -> field
  --/|
  --/| In this tutorial:
  --/| speeds chooses the Speed collection.
  --/| Walk chooses the Walk record.
  --/| valueFormula->flatValue chooses the number stored on that record.
  --/|
  --/| READING AND WRITING USE THE SAME PATH
  --/| The read is:
  --/| [*S:speeds->Walk->valueFormula->flatValue]
  --/|
  --/| The write uses the same path with the Beacon !c command.
  --/|
  --/| WHY !c?
  --/| Typed collection fields are native Beacon record data. In Beacon mode,
  --/| !c is the command used for existing native, structured, and typed values.
  --/|
  --/| !a is not used for a typed path. Its Beacon role is attribute-style
  --/| compatibility, custom user. values, and familiar repeating-field syntax.
  --/|
  --/| IMPORTANT SAFETY RULE
  --/| A typed write changes an EXISTING primitive field: a string, number,
  --/| boolean, or other simple stored value.
  --/|
  --/| Do not use an ordinary typed write to replace an entire object or array.
  --/| Do not casually change identity or relationship fields such as shortID,
  --/| parentID, childIDs, or other IDs.
  --/|
  --/| The selector must identify one record. If a readable name matches more
  --/| than one record, use a unique shortID, canonical ID, or zero-based index.
  --/|
  --/| This tutorial restores both the original Walk speed and the original Poisoned
  --/| state before it finishes.
  --/| ==========================================================================

  --#beaconsheet|1
  --#sourcetoken|@{selected|token_id}
  --#title|[*S:character_name] - Typed Collection Write Tutorial

  --&CharacterID|[*S:character_id]

  --/| Save the current Walk speed. The default text lets us detect a character
  --/| that does not have a usable Walk record.
  --&OriginalWalk|[*S:speeds->Walk->valueFormula->flatValue:::No Walk record]

  --?"[&OriginalWalk]" -eq "No Walk record"|NoWalk

  --=TestWalk|[&OriginalWalk] + 1

  --+|[c][b]The Typed Collection Path[/b][/c]

  --+Collection|speeds
  --+Selector|Walk
  --+Field|valueFormula->flatValue
  --+Complete Path|speeds->Walk->valueFormula->flatValue

  --+|[c][b]Starting Value[/b][/c]

  --+Character|[*S:character_name]
  --+Walk Speed|[&OriginalWalk]

  --+|[c][b]Write the Typed Field[/b][/c]

  --/| !c locates the Walk record and changes only its existing flatValue field.
  --!c:[&CharacterID]|speeds->Walk->valueFormula->flatValue:[$TestWalk.Raw]

  --+Value Written|[$TestWalk.Raw]
  --+Value Read Back|[*S:speeds->Walk->valueFormula->flatValue]

  --+|[c][b]Restore the Original Value[/b][/c]

  --!c:[&CharacterID]|speeds->Walk->valueFormula->flatValue:[&OriginalWalk]

  --+Restored Walk Speed|[*S:speeds->Walk->valueFormula->flatValue]

  --+|[c][b]Boolean Typed Write: Condition _active[/b][/c]

  --/| Built-in Conditions use _active as their actual on/off state. _enabled is
  --/| different: it controls whether the canonical record participates in the
  --/| character model and is NOT the normal Condition toggle.
  --/|
  --/| Existing boolean leaves accept true/false, 1/0, yes/no, and on/off.
  --/| Invalid boolean text is rejected instead of being guessed.
  --/|
  --/| IMPORTANT: this example briefly changes the character's real Poisoned state.
  --/| If Condition/token synchronization is enabled, the token marker may change
  --/| too. The original state is restored immediately after the read-back.
  --&OriginalPoisoned|[*S:conditions->Poisoned->_active:::No Poisoned record]
  --?"[&OriginalPoisoned]" -eq "No Poisoned record"|SkipConditionExample

  --&TestPoisoned|true
  --?"[&OriginalPoisoned]" -eq "true"|[
    --&TestPoisoned|false
  --]|

  --+Starting Poisoned State|[&OriginalPoisoned]
  --!c:[&CharacterID]|conditions->Poisoned->_active:[&TestPoisoned]
  --+Temporary Poisoned State|[*S:conditions->Poisoned->_active]

  --/| Restore the exact original state.
  --!c:[&CharacterID]|conditions->Poisoned->_active:[&OriginalPoisoned]
  --+Restored Poisoned State|[*S:conditions->Poisoned->_active]
  --^AfterConditionExample|

  --:SkipConditionExample|
  --+Condition Example|Skipped because this character has no Poisoned Condition record.

  --:AfterConditionExample|
  --+|[c][b]What to Remember[/b][/c]

  --+Read|Use collection -> selector -> field inside a normal Beacon sheet reference.
  --+Write|Use the same typed path with Beacon !c when changing an existing primitive field.
  --+Selector|Use a unique name, ID, or index that identifies exactly one record.
  --+Boolean Fields|Existing boolean leaves accept normal boolean values; for Conditions, _active is the actual on/off state.
  --+Safety|Avoid identity fields, relationship fields, whole objects, and whole arrays unless you fully understand the record structure.

  --+|[c][b]Finished[/b][/c]

  --+Result|A numeric typed field and a boolean Condition state were changed through typed paths and restored.
  --X|

  --:NoWalk|
  --+|[c][b]No Walk Record Found[/b][/c]
  --+Result|Select a Beacon character with a Walk speed and run the tutorial again.
}}
